+## Near-optimal single-instance sorting under partial information (use with care)
+
+- B. Haeupler, R. Hladík, J. Iacono, V. Rozhoň, R. E. Tarjan, J. Tětek,
+  *Fast and Simple Sorting Using Partial Information*, Algorithmica 88(3)
+  (2026), DOI https://doi.org/10.1007/s00453-026-01387-3; preprint
+  arXiv:2404.04552v4 [cs.DS].
+  Given a DAG with `m` pre-existing comparison arcs and `T` topological
+  orders, a deterministic algorithm (topological heapsort with a
+  working-set-bounded pairing heap, plus a bottleneck/level lookahead step)
+  outputs a topological order in `O(m + log T)` time using `O(log T)`
+  comparisons, optimal up to constant factors. This is the **single-instance**
+  problem — sort one given poset — not the exact optimization `S(P)`, and not
+  the fixed-antichain value `S(n)`. The `O(log T)` comparison bound is not new
+  (the paper credits the polynomial-time `O(log T)`-comparison algorithm to
+  Cardinal et al., its [30]; the novelty is the `O(m + log T)` execution time).
+  Do not use this result to claim exact `S(P)` or exact `S(n)`; the exact
+  minimum requires the exponential minimax recurrence.
